  "P2": "c01/step-03\ncheck_consistency: 1 command(s) break a consistency boundary: fix each before finishing the step.\n\n1. src/sales/application/PlaceOrder.ts:24 [one aggregate per command; cross-context write]\n   Rule: One command changes and saves one aggregate instance. Any other aggregate (another instance of the same type, or another type) is updated afterwards by a handler of a domain event that the first aggregate records. A bounded context changes only its own aggregates. Another context's aggregate changes when that context reacts to an event it subscribes to, not inside this command.\n   Why: PlaceOrder.execute (src/sales/application/PlaceOrder.ts:24) saves more than one aggregate instance in the same call: Order: order; Member: member. PlaceOrder.execute (src/sales/application/PlaceOrder.ts:24), in context sales, changes Member (owned by membership) through PlaceOrder.execute → MembershipDirectory.awardPoints → awardPoints (published interface) → AwardPoints.execute → MemberRepository.save.\n   Write: src/membership/application/AwardPoints.ts:12 MemberRepository.save(…) via PlaceOrder.execute → MembershipDirectory.awardPoints → awardPoints (published interface) → AwardPoints.execute → MemberRepository.save\n   Write: src/sales/application/PlaceOrder.ts:32 OrderRepository.save(…) via PlaceOrder.execute → OrderRepository.save\n   Fix: Do not call the other context's write operation from this command. Record a domain event in Order (saved with it), and let membership subscribe to that event through its published interface and change its own aggregate there.",
  "P1": "c10/step-03\ncheck_consistency: 1 command(s) break a consistency boundary: fix each before finishing the step.\n\n1. src/ledger/application/Transfer.ts:18 [one aggregate per command]\n   Rule: One command changes and saves one aggregate instance. Any other aggregate (another instance of the same type, or another type) is updated afterwards by a handler of a domain event that the first aggregate records.\n   Why: Transfer.execute (src/ledger/application/Transfer.ts:18) saves more than one aggregate instance in the same call: Account: from, to.\n   Write: src/ledger/application/Transfer.ts:35 AccountRepository.saveAll(…) via Transfer.execute → AccountRepository.saveAll\n   Fix: Let this command change and save one Account. Put the change of the other Account in a method that records a domain event, and apply it to the other instance in a handler of that event (idempotent per event), so each command writes one instance."
 },
